\ =====================================================================
\ ffsh prelude
\ ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Convenience words that ship with the *ffsh* shell, written in Forth
\ rather than baked into the engine. ffsh evaluates this file once at
\ startup, before the first prompt, so every word defined here is
\ available in an interactive session.
\
\ This file is deliberately part of the *shell*, not the *ff* library.
\ The engine has no built-in Forth and no global state; anything that
\ can be expressed as a Forth definition lives here instead, which is
\ the whole point of the parsing words (`parse-word`, `parse`) and
\ `postpone`: new notation is a library, not a language change. Edit
\ this file (it is embedded at build time) to add your own shortcuts.
\ =====================================================================


\ ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\ char / [char] --- character literals
\ ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\ *ff*'s `parse ( char -- c-addr )` takes its delimiter as a character
\ *code*, e.g. `41 parse` to read up to ')'. Writing raw ASCII codes is
\ error-prone; `char` and `[char]` turn a character written literally in
\ the source into its code, so you can write `char )` instead of `41`.
\
\ Two words are needed for the same reason `'` has the companion `[']`
\ and why `literal` exists: a value produced while *interpreting* must be
\ explicitly compiled in when it is wanted *inside a definition*.
\
\   - `char`   acts at interpret time and pushes the code now.
\   - `[char]` acts at compile time: it is immediate, so when the word
\              being defined is compiled it lays the code down as a
\              literal that will be pushed when that word later runs.
\
\ Both read their character with `parse-word` (the next whitespace token
\ as text) and take its first byte with `c@`. `[char]` additionally uses
\ `postpone literal` so the literal is compiled when `[char]` *runs*,
\ not while `[char]` itself is being defined (ff's `literal` is itself
\ immediate and would otherwise fire one level too early).
\
\ Caveat: because they read through the tokenizer, `char` / `[char]`
\ cannot capture characters that are lexically special — `(` (opens a
\ comment), `"` (opens a string), `\` (line comment), or whitespace. For
\ those, use the numeric code directly (e.g. `40` for '(' ). Every
\ ordinary character works: letters, digits, `)` `+` `-` `]` `.` etc.

\ char ( "name" -- c )  Push the code of the first character of the next
\ whitespace-delimited token.  e.g.  char A .   →  65
: char    parse-word c@ ;

\ [char] ( "name" -- c )  Compile-time char: inside a definition, compile
\ the code of the next token's first character as a literal.
\   e.g.  : open-paren  [char] ( ;    open-paren .   →  40
: [char]  parse-word c@ postpone literal ; immediate


